course: dictionary
lesson: 5775
title: Words that move: lurch
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Lurch is a word about movement. When something lurches, it moves suddenly and in a rough, unsteady way. The words around it give you clues about how it moved. «The bus braked hard, and everyone lurched forward.» The bus stopped fast, so the people were thrown forward. That is what lurch means.
explain: Lurch can also be a noun, meaning one sudden jerky movement. «The car gave a lurch as it started.» Here a lurch is one big jolt. Lurch can also be a feeling: «My stomach lurched» means you suddenly felt a scary jump inside, like when you miss a step on the stairs.
explain: The phrase «leave someone in the lurch» is different. It means to leave someone alone when they need help. If your friend needs you for a project and you go home, you have left them in the lurch. Here you cannot work out the meaning from the movement; you need the whole phrase.
fact: Lurch means to move suddenly and unsteadily.
fact: «The ship lurched in the storm» tells you the ship swayed and jerked, not that it moved smoothly.
fact: «With a lurch» means with a sudden, jerky movement.
fact: When a bus brakes suddenly, the people inside lurch forward, because their bodies keep moving even after the bus has stopped.
fact: «Leave someone in the lurch» means to leave them alone when they need help.
exercise: «The bus braked suddenly, and the passengers lurched forward.» What does lurched mean? | Stood very still | Sang loudly | Fell fast asleep | *Moved suddenly and unsteadily
exercise: «The ship lurched in the storm.» Find and copy one word that means moved suddenly and unsteadily. | *lurched | ship | the | storm
exercise: Nadia says, «My stomach lurched when I saw the spider.» What does it mean? | I felt very full after lunch | *I suddenly felt a scary jump inside | My stomach was rumbling because I was hungry | I was eating my lunch too fast
test: Dev's friend had a big project due, and Dev went home without helping. Dev left his friend in the lurch. What does that phrase mean? | Gave his friend a present | Helped his friend finish the project | Raced his friend home | *Left his friend alone when he needed help
test: Which sentence uses lurch as a noun, meaning one sudden jerky movement? | *The lift gave a sudden lurch. | The lift lurched upwards. | The lift is lurching slowly.
exam: Why does your body lurch forward when a bus brakes suddenly? | You were trying to reach the door early | Your seat pushes you forward on purpose | The bus pulls you forward with a rope | *Your body keeps moving the way it was moving until something stops it
